  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 306 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe long term period picks up on Saturday night with a subtropical \nlow centered off the Georgia coast. This low is expected to continue \nnortheastward, potentially gaining some tropical characteristics as \nit evolves and strengthens. Aside from isolated light showers in \nportions of east Georgia Saturday night into Sunday morning, no \ndirect impacts are expected in north and central Georgia. Low \ntemperatures on Sunday morning are forecast to be 6-12 degrees above \nnormal, ranging from the mid 40s in the far northern mountains to \nthe mid 50s in east-central Georgia. Drier air on the back side of \nthe low will promote benign weather conditions and clearing skies on \nSunday. Highs on Sunday will be relatively warm for late November, \nin the low to mid 70s and between 8-14 degrees warmer than daily \nnormals.\n\nSunday night into Monday, a mid level shortwave will move across the \nTennessee Valley region, which will help advance a cold front \ntowards northwest Georgia. Cloud cover will increase ahead of this \nfront, though forcing will be limited as the front weakens and \nbecomes more diffuse and coverage of showers will be isolated, as a \nresult. After beginning the morning in the low 50s, highs will \nreflect the progression of the front on Monday afternoon, with 60s \nto the north of I-85 and in the mid to upper 70s in areas to the \nsouth. The progression of this front will begin to slow late in the \nday on Monday, eventually stalling in central Georgia. An open wave \ndeveloping near the south Texas Gulf coast will move across the \ncentral Gulf coast on Monday night and across south/central Georgia \non Tuesday. Scattered showers are forecast in the southern half of \nthe area during the daytime on Tuesday as this disturbance overruns \nthe lingering boundary.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday morning, a strong (1040 mb) surface \nhigh is progged to move east of the Great Lakes, with a potent CAD \nwedge developing along the lee side of the Appalachians and \nspreading into Georgia. Low temperatures on Wednesday morning will \nrange from the mid 30s in the far northeastern mountains to the mid \n40s in central Georgia. Mid-range models meanwhile indicate a deep \ntrough over the Great Lakes developing into a closed low as it moves \ntowards the Southeast. There is still ample disagreement on the \nposition and movement of this low, but it will nonetheless likely \nspread southwesterly mid and upper level flow and deep moisture over \nGeorgia. The combination of the wedge and this moist flow will \nlikely lead to a couple of raw days over the northern part of the \nstate, with cold rain and temperatures struggling to get above 50.",
